#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082985 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083172 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a81e85da-c4ce-5353-9a7b-d18daa4caf7d" :knowledge-point/title "Converting Between Exponential and Logarithmic Form With Common Logarithms"}] :question/id #uuid "057534f6-5144-57c1-ade0-1693dffa44e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-4058" :question/problem "Express the equation $10^3 = 1\\,000$ using logarithmic notation." :question/worked-solution "The common logarithm of $a$ is defined as follows:\n\n> $a = 10^x$ is equivalent to $\\log{a} = x$\n\nIf we compare\n\n$$\n10^3 = 1\\,000\n$$\n\nwith $10^x = a$, we get that $a = 1\\,000$ and $x = 3$.\n\nTherefore, the given equation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\log{1\\,000} = 3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082986 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083173 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "afb7f5bb-1a71-5bf5-a4a0-1354256be22b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Common Logarithms"}] :question/id #uuid "e9e02baa-a9bc-5f3f-af29-2bbac1dc2634" :question/math-academy-id "e-4059" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\log\\left(10^{7}\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "The common logarithm of a power of $10$ is the power itself:\n\n$$\n\\log\\left(10^x\\right) = x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x =7$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\log\\left(10^{7}\\right) = 7\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082987 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083174 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67d294d0-542a-5332-b837-256792817dde" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Common Logarithms of Fractions and Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "34a604f1-d3e2-5746-818a-17c572ff2243" :question/math-academy-id "e-4060" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\log\\left(0.1 \\right)$." :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite $0.1$ as a power of $10$ (the base of the common logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n0.1 = 10^{-1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\log\\left(0.1 \\right) = \\log\\left(10^{-1}\\right)\n$$\n\nThe common logarithm of a power of $10$ is the power itself:\n\n$$\n\\log\\left(10^x\\right) = x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x = -1$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\log\\left(10^{-1} \\right)= -1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082988 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083175 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60270e0c-a60c-5a5f-8993-3d8362081c8f" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Expressions Containing Common Logarithms Using a Calculator"}] :question/id #uuid "b4b8a7af-fe7c-5d8a-80f4-c997c404a889" :question/math-academy-id "e-4061" :question/problem "Rounded to $2$ decimal places, what is the value of $2\\log(7) + 5\\log(5)$?" :question/worked-solution "We compute the required expression to $2$ decimal places using a calculator, rounding the intermediate steps to $5$ decimal places to avoid rounding error. This gives\n\n$$\n2\\log (7) + 5\\log (5) \\mid \\approx 2(0.84510) + 5(0.69897); = 1.69020 + 3.49485; = 5.18505; \\approx 5.19\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233673 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083173 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "afb7f5bb-1a71-5bf5-a4a0-1354256be22b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Common Logarithms"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dbc275e8-af27-549a-94fa-e61e97b3469b" :question/math-academy-id "q-24882" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{log}({t}^{2})+\\operatorname{log}(10t)$ at $t=10.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $t=10$ into the function. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}({t}^{2})+\\operatorname{log}(10t) & =\\operatorname{log}({10}^{2})+\\operatorname{log}(10\\cdot 10) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}({10}^{2})+\\operatorname{log}({10}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}({10}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=2.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}({10}^{2})+\\operatorname{log}({10}^{2})=2+2=4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234203 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083172 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a81e85da-c4ce-5353-9a7b-d18daa4caf7d" :knowledge-point/title "Converting Between Exponential and Logarithmic Form With Common Logarithms"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2f9714aa-ab20-5f59-96d2-702c693f996b" :question/math-academy-id "q-66543" :question/problem "${10}^{-2}=\\frac{1}{100}$ can be expressed as" :question/worked-solution "The common logarithm of $a$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a={10}^{x}$ is equivalent to $\\operatorname{log}a=x$\n\nIf we compare ${10}^{-2}=\\frac{1}{100}$ with ${10}^{x}=a,$ we get that $a=\\frac{1}{100}$ and $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation can be expressed as $\\operatorname{log}(\\frac{1}{100})=-2.$"}]}
